# Commit Message Format

When creating git commits, always use the **Conventional Commits** format:

```
<keyword>: <one-sentence description>
```

## Keywords

- `feat` — A new feature
- `fix` — A bug fix
- `refactor` — Code restructuring without behavior change
- `chore` — Build, tooling, or dependency updates
- `docs` — Documentation changes only
- `style` — Formatting, whitespace, or code style (no logic change)
- `test` — Adding or updating tests
- `perf` — Performance improvement
- `ci` — CI/CD configuration changes

## Rules

- The description must be a single, concise English sentence in lowercase (except proper nouns).
- Do NOT capitalize the first letter of the description.
- Do NOT end the description with a period.
- The keyword must be one of the listed keywords above.
- Use the imperative mood (e.g., "add" not "added" or "adds").
- Keep the entire message under 72 characters when possible.

## Examples

- `feat: add clipboard history search functionality`
- `fix: resolve hotkey detection on macOS Sequoia`
- `refactor: extract streaming logic into shared utility`
- `chore: update tauri dependencies to v2.1`
- `docs: add architecture diagram to README`
- `test: add unit tests for glossary store`
